# Frozen exploratory implementation

Version 1.0.0, 7 October 2026. The numerical choices below are fixed in the
package before this run. Related results have already been viewed, including
prior construal splits and receipt cost interactions. This is an exploratory
extension, not a preregistration. The model is allowed to fail or produce
unclassified profiles; it never assigns a psychological label automatically.

## Population, orientation and dates

The input is the certified pool/nonadjacent 24-month main choice panel,
41,534,331 rows before this run's author exclusion. Its authority, every
monthly hash and row count are pinned. The main predicate is `source='pool'`
and `previous_classification_status='not_mutually_adjacent_observed'`.
Both roles of `__PRIVATE_VALUE_1__` and `__PRIVATE_VALUE_2__` are excluded before histories, markers
and estimates. No additional analytical row deletions are silently allowed.

All five states describe the **disconnected recipient**, not the chooser.
A competitive opportunity means recipient evaluation >= -100 cp. Mate scores
retain the certified finite score convention; mate indicators are retained.

| State | Recipient evaluation |
|---|---|
| Clearly lost (0) | cp <= -300 |
| Behind (1) | -300 < cp < -100 |
| Roughly equal (2) | -100 <= cp <= 100 |
| Ahead (3) | 100 < cp < 300 |
| Well ahead (4) | cp >= 300, including the corresponding winning-mate scores |

Year 1: [2023-11-01, 2024-11-01). Year 2: [2024-11-01, 2025-11-01).
UTC throughout. Stage upper bounds in plies: 2, 4, 6, 10, 20, 40, 80; last
band >80. Clock bands: <10, [10,30), [30,60), [60,120), >=120 seconds;
missing/negative clocks remain a separate descriptive category.

## Build and histories

One slim monthly projection feeds all available analyses. Account/state/year
counts feed B1 without expanding accounts into repeated observations.
Prior screens, all-state grants, competitive grants, receipts and claimed
losses while ahead are accumulated jointly over both roles. Claimed while
winning uses recipient cp >= +100. An event is usable only when its recorded
end plus 300 seconds is strictly before the next focal game's start. Exact
end-time ties are aggregated before cumulative sums. Last event times are
retained for elapsed-day controls. C1 additionally drops ambiguous overlapping
opportunities; its sample flow reports those exclusions.

These are histories of eligible opportunities in the observed main panel,
not UI telemetry, all-source histories, or account-lifetime histories. The
first observed opportunity is not necessarily an account's first opportunity
on Lichess. The account can have grants before November 2023 or outside the
pool/nonadjacent frame. This limits conversion/learning interpretations.

Available account markers are constructed without using grant decisions:
ratings at main opportunities, annual SDs, primary-speed versions of those
rating proxies, account age if known, and Patron status from the authenticated
later profile snapshot. The primary speed uses opportunity counts, with
alphabetical tie-breaking. A mixed-speed SD can mix distinct rating pools;
use the primary-speed proxy in preference to it. Neither equals an all-game
annual SD. Patron coverage is selected and later-dated, so its missingness
and coverage are reported by type. Missing all-game markers are not zeros.

## E: lost-position diagnostics

E1 compares raw Year-1/Year-2 grant-rate correlations on >=1 eligible
opportunity in each year and >=4 total, separately for lost and competitive
positions. It also reports harmonic-exposure-weighted correlations, >=5/20
per-year sensitivities, and 200 account-bootstrap intervals for the raw
correlation. Few opportunities and rare grants attenuate raw correlations;
a precise value near zero does not itself prove noise.

E2 reports lost-position grant rates by speed, clock band and year, with
chooser-cluster delta-method SEs. Clock is at the final recorded move, not
at the actual click. These are clues about inattention, not observed misclicks.

E3 selects accounts with >=2 lost-position grants **in Year 1 only** and
reports their profiles across all states in both years. Year 2 is the useful
held-out shape; selection on Year-1 outcomes makes its in-sample profile
mechanically selected.

E4/G2 use PR 17866's July 24, 2025 merge as a date proxy, +/-56 days, weekly
event time, reference week -1, and cutoff shifts -7/0/+7 days. Blitz, rapid
and classical contrasts are relative to bullet/ultrabullet; the joint
chooser-cluster covariance includes all four cells of each difference.
All speed groups changed. There is no untreated group. The source diff
changes only RoundSocket.scala, not button layout. Grace periods change
from 40 to 30 seconds for bullet, 40 to 60 blitz, 80 to 120 rapid and 120 to
300 classical. Live deployment is not independently verified. Composition
tables report state, stage, ratings, cost and clocks among observed events;
they cannot count prevented disconnections or reconnections.

Source: https://github.com/lichess-org/lila/pull/17866 . The downloaded PR
metadata and file diff are bundled in authority/. Merge UTC is
2025-07-24T06:24:50Z, SHA 14ce8cd641d49151c3db67ff8711ddce9d67d3d4.

## B: account mixtures and validation

Year-1 thresholds: M=20 main, M=50 and M=4 sensitivities. Count all five
states toward M. B1 fits unrestricted account-level binomial mixtures by
log-sum-exp EM for K=1..6, 20 dispersed starts each, up to 1,000 iterations,
relative likelihood tolerance 1e-8 and probability floor 1e-10. An initially
near-zero component is allowed but is not constrained to remain zero.
Probabilities are never constrained to increase, flatten or peak at equality.
The common binomial combinatorial term is omitted consistently across K.

BIC uses the number of accounts, with 6K-1 parameters. ICL = BIC + twice
posterior entropy. Normalized entropy is entropy/(N log K), defined as zero
for K=1. Confidence is the share of accounts with max posterior >0.9. Select
minimum BIC among converged fits whose every share >=0.001; lower K wins an
exact tie. The full convergence/BIC/ICL/entropy path remains in the report.
Small or unsupported components are visible, not silently psychological types.

B2 uses B1's selected K for each M. It maximizes the exact marginal account
likelihood by analytic-gradient L-BFGS-B, with type-specific state intercepts
and standardized-kappa slopes, common stage/speed/month coefficients, and 20
starts. Kappa mean and SD come only from the eligible Year-1 sample. Numerical
bounds +/-25, convergence diagnostics, bound hits and component shares are
reported. This is a mixture logit, not a second ordinary pooled regression.
Profiles standardize nuisance covariates to a deterministic outcome-free
sample of at most 100,000 eligible Year-1 rows, at mean Year-1 cost.

For the main M=20 Year-1 fit, B1 and B2 each have 200 account bootstrap
replications, 3 starts per replication (fitted/warm start plus dispersed
alternatives), selected K held fixed. Match components to the full-data
profiles by minimum squared profile distance. Intervals are conditional on K;
they do not include model-selection uncertainty. At least 90% valid refits
are required for an AVAILABLE inference label. M=50/4 do not get separate
200-replication bootstraps. Original full-data fits always use 20 starts.

B3 assigns Year-1 modal types and reports Year-2 rates by state, account
retention, marker means/SEs with observed counts, and type counts by primary
Year-1 speed. Re-estimate B1 independently in Year 2 for all three M values.
Comparison/alignment of Year-2 profiles never updates Year-1 assignments.
Rate SEs cluster by account conditional on assignments; marker-mean SEs treat
the fitted labels as fixed. Assignment uncertainty is not included in these
particular descriptive SEs. Locally export both posteriors and merged
account/type/marker Parquet files, omitted from the return ZIP.

A rising profile with a negative cost slope is compatible with selective
accommodation; a flat profile is compatible with several mechanisms. A
near-zero type is not an identified structural never-granter. Label after
examining coverage, uncertainty and validation, and retain numeric labels
where the data do not discriminate.

## C and H: hazard and receipt

C1 is a logit hazard at competitive choices until the first observed
competitive grant. It includes log(1+prior screens), prior receipt indicator
and log(1+days since), prior claimed-while-winning indicator and recency,
state, kappa, stage, speed, current month, log(1+tenure), tenure missingness,
rating/100, and first-observed-opportunity-month effects. SEs cluster by
chooser. Exposure-decile plots show raw at-risk event rates with cluster SEs;
ties are preserved. The specification is across the observed panel, not
within session. Controls do not eliminate dynamic selection into survival,
activity or unobserved lifetime history. Exposure associations are not causal
estimates of learning or construal.

C2/C3 authenticate the completed receipt-mechanisms cache 08b0e91f184f and
original IV source, retain its 2,682,329-row cohort, and verify game, role,
treatment, position and end-time identity against the main panel. No source
cohort is reconstructed from game outcomes. The treatment is received draw;
the instrument is the existing adjusted_7d pooled prior-leniency score,
scaled by its fixed reference SD 0.059411690344000595. Include the original
15 rich controls and fixed effects; do not include the excluded score as a
control. Interact all included controls and FEs with the subgroup and
instrument both subgroup treatment interactions. The two coefficients and
their difference use one joint account-or-exposure-date CR0 covariance.

C2 splits zero versus positive **prior observed all-state grants**. C3 splits
zero versus positive prior observed opportunities. Outcome Y is a kind act
at the next different-opponent competitive choice within 90 days; nonreachers
are zero. The reached-opportunity outcome is reported alongside it. A split
needs >=2,000 rows, >=10 receipts and >=10 kind events in each group; otherwise
it is withheld with its counts. Report point estimates/CIs in pp and events
per 1,000 receipts, covariance of the contrast, first stages and conditional
F diagnostics. These conventional intervals are not weak-IV robust. Conditional
exclusion is an assumption, not certified by the subgroup design. Positive
Y effects can reflect opportunity reach as well as choice propensity.

H plots raw and adjusted score-decile first stages and reduced forms, keeping
score ties together; adjusted contrasts use the lowest bin as reference and
the same rich controls/FE and covariance rule. Dynamic IV outcomes count
kind acts and competitive opportunities against different opponents in
(0,7], (7,30], (30,90] days. All focal rows have complete 90-day follow-up.
Report counts per 1,000 receipts and per day because the windows differ in
length. These are not conditional grant rates; separate CIs do not test a
cross-window difference.

H's environment-controls table is copied from the hash-verified earlier
report cea0b052e5f2 for XC and XC_both_rates, keeping original cohorts,
instruments, support, diagnostics and intervals. It is explicitly archived,
not a newly fitted environment-controls version of C2. G1 adds a joint
receipt-Year split and year-by-year main descriptive results. Other paper
headlines are not claimed to be rerun by this package.

## D and F: estimators conditional on new inputs

D1 requires ordinary-game continuation cells: chooser cp and rating gap bins
of 50, the same stage bands, speed, and at least 100 distinct games per cell.
Exclude mate-scored main opportunities from this finite-cp calibration. It
runs an all-state and an equal-position chooser-FE LPM using 1-p(win), stage,
state when applicable, 2-point kappa bins (last bin >=18), speed and month.
Reference categories are the lowest observed category within the retained
sample. Report coverage before estimates and equal-position p(win) profiles.
SEs condition on the estimated calibration. A final mechanism claim would
need calibration uncertainty propagated by game/account resampling.
Empirical p(win) is not a measurement of subjective expectations. A conditional
stage effect alone does not constitute a test that rejects all KR accounts.

D2 requires complete-game ACPL for each player, not the last four plies. It
absorbs chooser and rounded-integer-eval x exact-ply fixed effects and
estimates both players' ACPL terms. Negative recipient ACPL slope means
more granting to better measured play. It remains an observational association.

F uses outoftime losers with last recorded clock >=30 seconds blitz,
60 rapid or 120 classical, and no bullet threshold. Combine these proxy
waiters with active timeout choices of matching scope. Estimate state rates
and a chooser-FE LPM. A probable abandoner is not evidence that the chooser
actually saw a button or deliberately waited. F2 reports both all-waiter
0%/100% scenarios and the logically wider lower/upper gap bounds obtained by
opposite assignments in competitive and lost states. Bounds apply to this
augmented proxy frame, not every possible disconnection.

## Decision language

Use convergent patterns to motivate behavioral descriptions, not a mechanical
accept/reject tree over mental states. A null effect needs a narrow confidence
interval around a substantively small range; failure to reject is not
similarity. A zero-history receipt effect is a response among accounts with
no previously observed grant, not proof that non-construers were converted.
A prior-granter effect is consistent with intensification but does not alone
identify a fixed type. Keep the neutral receipt wording until mechanism
comparisons support something stronger.
